= Oncology bibliography

= Leukaemia by Cancer Council Australia
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cancer.org.au/types-of-cancer/leukaemia

Definition, classification and symptoms. The current disease classification is distinguished from the 1864 descriptive label.

= Neoplasia by the University of Utah WebPath
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://webpath.med.utah.edu/NEOHTML/NEOPL107.html

Malignant Neoplasms: cell morphology, prominent nucleoli, mitoses and epithelial versus mesenchymal classification.

= Illustrations of the Pathology of Cancer by John Zachariah Laurence
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1856}
{tag=Work by John Zachariah Laurence}

https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Illustrations_of_the_pathology_of_cancer_%28IA_b22308052%29.pdf

1856; pp. 35–37 describe fibres, elongated nuclei and fibroplastic cells and discuss recurrence or malignant behaviour. Publication and author verified against https://wellcomecollection.org/works/wmmt58cd. The historical category is not treated as a unique present diagnosis.

= Recurrent Breast Cancer by NCI
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cancer.gov/types/breast/breast-cancer-survivorship/recurrence

Current patient information headed Breast Cancer Recurrence: local, regional and distant return, and factors affecting recurrence risk.

= Cancer treatment discussion in the British Medical Journal (17 November 1866)
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1866}

https://www.bmj.com/content/bmj/2/307/548.full.pdf

Pages 548–549; contemporary discussion of caustics, chemical injection and purported prevention of recurrence. Cited as evidence of what was proposed, not as support for those treatments.

= On Cancer: Its Allies and Counterfeits by Thomas Weeden Cooke
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1865}

https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/On_cancer_-_its_allies_and_counterfeits_%28IA_b21950039%29.pdf

<Thomas Weeden Cooke>, pp. 131–135, nonoperative case records. Period observations of palliation or altered symptoms are not evidence of eradication of cancer.

= Early and locally advanced breast cancer by NICE
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng101/chapter/Recommendations

NG101; pretreatment assessment, surgery, systemic therapy planning and adjuvant treatment.

= How Is Breast Cancer Diagnosed? by NCI
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cancer.gov/types/breast/diagnosis

Imaging, core needle biopsy, pathology, biomarkers and staging.

= Breast Cancer Signs and Symptoms by NCI
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2025}

https://www.cancer.gov/types/breast/symptoms

Lumps, nipple and skin changes, pain and signs of distant disease.

= Leukemia by the American Cancer Society
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/leukemia.html

How leukemia starts; Features and symptoms, acute versus chronic disease.

= Leukemia by MedlinePlus
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://medlineplus.gov/leukemia.html

What is leukemia?; Symptoms; Diagnosis. Official National Library of Medicine overview.

= Overview of Leukemia by Merck Manual Professional Edition
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/oncology/leukemias/overview-of-leukemia

Introduction; Classification; Findings at Diagnosis table; Leukemoid reaction. Review February 2026.

= Nausea and Vomiting Related to Cancer Treatment by the National Cancer Institute
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects/nausea/nausea-hp-pdq

PDQ Health Professional Version; “Pathophysiology of N&V” describes visceral afferent pathways and coordinated efferent responses.

= What Is Cancer? by the National Cancer Institute
{parent=Oncology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/understanding/what-is-cancer

Sections on types of cancer, including carcinoma, sarcoma, leukaemia, lymphoma and melanoma.

